- Duplicate tool calls are allowed only for the following tool names: {{ duplicate_tools }}. Only these tool names may repeat with identical {tool, args}; adjust arguments for every other tool if you need new data.
{% else -%}
- Duplicate tool calls are not allowed. Never repeat an identical {tool, args} pair; adjust arguments if you need new data.
{% endif %}
{% if parallelizable -%}
- You may parallelize tool calls that do not depend on each other (e.g., `get_method_details` for multiple methods).
{% else -%}
- Do not parallelize tool calls. Use sequential calls. Await results of previous tools before calling dependent tools.
{% endif %}

# ITERATION LIMIT
- You must complete within at least {{ min_iters }} model step(s) (iterations). If you reach this limit, finalize immediately with your best possible localized scenario.

# GUIDELINES AND TIPS
- Start by using the method and class search tools, with their vector databases, to retrieve the most relevant methods and classes for each step.
- Use code metadata tools to understand the codebase and determine method/class relevance.
- For each step, identify one to three candidate methods (either library or external methods), ordered by preference with the best candidate first.
- Refine the initial decomposition into the best step set for faithful execution. Merge, split, reorder, or remove steps as needed. If the code indicates more steps should be needed to execute a desired behavior, introduce and localize those steps in the same scenario format. Merge steps that can be executed as a single method call.
- Verify each candidate with tool evidence.
- Use `get_inherited_library_classes` to identify inherited library classes and consequent callable library methods on a class, if the application methods are not sufficient.
- Aim for each step to map cleanly to 1 concrete best method when possible.
- If a step cannot be localized and has no clear justification, remove it from the scenario. An example of a valid non-localized step to remain in the scenario, with a note explaining why it is not localized, is a step that instantiates a primitive variable.

# END CONDITIONS
Your task is complete when:
- You understand the application code and have sufficient tool evidence to localize all steps.
- Each step in the description has one to three candidate methods available, are removed, or are marked as non-localized through notes.
OR you have run out of iterations.